159
00:16:51.008 --> 00:17:00.153
<v Daniel>Right. So alpha helices are those classic repeating corkscrew shapes you see in protein structures. They're absolutely crucial for how proteins fold and function in living organisms.</v>

160
00:17:00.494 --> 00:17:06.497
<v Maya>So the AI figured out how to mathematically command another AI to generate more of that specific physical structure.</v>

161
00:17:06.597 --> 00:17:08.098
<v Daniel>Exactly. Completely hands off.</v>

162
00:17:08.258 --> 00:17:16.903
<v Maya>Okay. So let's pause and look at where we are. We have Spark to Paper, an AI that perfectly formats, cites, and writes the research document.</v>

163
00:17:17.902 --> 00:17:32.508
<v Daniel>We have Mechanist, an AI that navigates 43 million papers to discover safety loopholes, pinpoint the neurological belief centers of other AIs, and generate biological structures. That's a lot. Have we done it? Are human scientists</v>

164
00:17:32.628 --> 00:17:34.969
<v Daniel>obsolete? Let's drop the ultimate reality check.</v>

165
00:17:35.590 --> 00:17:45.774
<v Maya>It is definitely time for the tension in our deep dive. The third paper we are looking at is titled, Can AI Agents Conduct Open-Ended AI Research? Early Evidence from Two Case Studies.</v>

166
00:17:46.085 --> 00:17:56.530
<v Daniel>And this comes from Peter Kyrgios, Sayash Kapoor, Arvind Narayanan, Helen Toner, Gillian Hadfield, and a large team at Princeton, the UK AI Security Institute, and CSET.</v>

167
00:17:57.010 --> 00:17:59.911
<v Maya>Yes. And they ran what they call shadow evaluations.</v>

168
00:18:00.051 --> 00:18:06.874
<v Daniel>I love this setup. Here's what they did. They took the central research question from a real, unpublished NeurIPS 2026 submission.</v>

169
00:18:08.522 --> 00:18:09.683
<v Maya>So a totally novel question.</v>

170
00:18:09.723 --> 00:18:23.189
<v Daniel>Totally novel. They gave this question to a frontier AI agent, gave it a budget of $3,000 to compute and six full days to work on it. Yeah. Then they had the original human authors of the real paper grade, the AI's final output as blind reviewers.</v>

171
00:18:23.209 --> 00:18:27.791
<v Maya>The AI had everything it needed. It had the prompt, the budget, the time. And what happened?</v>

172
00:18:28.252 --> 00:18:38.628
<v Daniel>The result was an epic, unambiguous failure. The original authors rejected the AI's attempts with highly competent scores of 2 out of 6 and 1 out of 6.</v>

173
00:18:38.828 --> 00:18:45.130
<v Maya>Ouch. An absolute rejection. But why? If these AI systems are so smart, what went wrong?</v>

174
00:18:45.805 --> 00:18:52.632
<v Daniel>Well, if we connect this to the bigger picture, the reasons why the AI failed are far more important than the scores themselves.</v>

175
00:18:52.792 --> 00:18:53.032
<v Maya>Right.</v>

176
00:18:53.472 --> 00:19:07.546
<v Daniel>The agents did all the engineering unaided, but they failed on five critical human dimensions of research. First, they had absolutely no judgment regarding the bar for publishable work. They literally couldn't tell if their finding was actually interesting.</v>

177
00:19:07.566 --> 00:19:08.507
<v Maya>They just don't have taste.</v>

178
00:19:08.967 --> 00:19:16.588
<v Daniel>Exactly. Second, they had no sense of their own budget. Both agents used less than half of the $3,000 allocated to them. They just stopped early.</v>

179
00:19:17.153 --> 00:19:25.720
<v Maya>Which is wild. Any human PhD student given three grand of compute would burn every single cent of it, running more seeds, larger models, and triple checking their data.</v>

180
00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:32.565
<v Daniel>Precisely. Third, the agents showed zero creative response to criticism. If they hit a wall, they just tried the exact same thing again.</v>

181
00:19:32.685 --> 00:19:33.305
<v Maya>So stubborn.</v>

182
00:19:33.546 --> 00:19:45.903
<v Daniel>Fourth, they showed no real backtracking. After the first 10 hours of work, if they were on the wrong path, they just stubbornly stayed on it for the next five days. They couldn't zoom out and realize the premise was flawed.</v>

183
00:19:46.063 --> 00:19:47.104
<v Maya>Just stuck in a rut.</v>

184
00:19:47.284 --> 00:19:56.808
<v Daniel>Yeah. And finally, they suffered from profound instruction drift. Over six days, they simply forgot what explicit instructions they were supposed to be following.</v>

185
00:19:56.988 --> 00:20:03.432
<v Maya>And even when the researchers swapped out the underlying model and changed the scaffolding, the exact same failures reproduce.</v>

186
00:20:03.472 --> 00:20:04.912
<v Daniel>It didn't matter what model they used.</v>

187
00:20:04.932 --> 00:20:14.559
<v Maya>So I have to ask you directly. Are Spark to Paper and Mechanist actually succeeding at automating science? Or are they just measuring the parts of science that are easy to measure?</v>

188
00:20:14.719 --> 00:20:15.940
<v Daniel>That's the million-dollar question.</v>

189
00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:30.830
<v Maya>Right. I mean, Spark to Paper measures citation validity and figure editability. Mechanist measures hypothesis, novelty, and reliability based on human judges evaluating the steps. But the shadow evaluation measures whether the authors of the actual paper think you actually did the</v>

190
00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:31.270
<v Maya>research.</v>

191
00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:46.517
<v Daniel>It is a vital distinction. Spark to Paper's own headline metrics are about the artifact, the latex compiling, the vector graphics, not the profundity of the discovery itself. Exactly. And if you read the Mechanist paper closely, even though it executes the</v>

192
00:20:46.537 --> 00:20:54.000
<v Daniel>experiments brilliantly, it still requires a human in the loop to set the initial scientific objectives and evaluation criteria.</v>

193
00:20:54.221 --> 00:20:59.486
<v Maya>The AI can drive the car flawlessly, but it still needs a human to tell it what city to drive to.</v>

194
00:20:59.746 --> 00:21:03.150
<v Daniel>Exactly. And this tension isn't new. It's been building all year.</v>

195
00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:03.870
<v Maya>Yeah, it has.</v>

196
00:21:04.211 --> 00:21:15.842
<v Daniel>Everyone is chasing the lineage established by Liu, Lange, Ha, and Klune back in March 2026 with the original AI scientist people in nature. Both spark-to-paper and mechanists benchmark themselves against it.</v>

197
00:21:16.085 --> 00:21:30.450
<v Maya>But there was another Nature paper recently by Haozhu, Lee, and Evans that explicitly warned us about this dynamic. Yes. They showed that while AI tools massively expand an individual scientist's raw output, they actively contract the range of questions that science asks.</v>

198
00:21:30.708 --> 00:21:36.130
<v Daniel>Because the AI gravitates toward the easily measurable, the highly cited, the mathematically neat problems?</v>

199
00:21:36.611 --> 00:21:42.894
<v Maya>Exactly. It avoids the messy, ambiguous, paradigm-shifting questions because it lacks intuition.</v>

200
00:21:43.014 --> 00:21:56.980
<v Daniel>Science is not just about executing a method perfectly. It's about recognizing anomaly, having a sense of aesthetics for what makes a problem elegant, and possessing the sheer stubbornness to completely backtrack after a month of failure.</v>

201
00:21:57.178 --> 00:21:58.659
<v Maya>which machines just don't have yet.</v>

202
00:21:58.879 --> 00:22:02.522
<v Daniel>The shadow evaluations prove that frontier agents currently lack all of that.</v>

203
00:22:03.142 --> 00:22:10.187
<v Maya>So what does this all mean? Let's synthesize this deep dive. We are living in a moment of incredible contradictory extremes.</v>

204
00:22:10.307 --> 00:22:10.887
<v Daniel>We really are.</v>

205
00:22:11.128 --> 00:22:25.737
<v Maya>On one hand, we have built systems like Spark to Paper that can compile flawless latex, generate native vector graphics, and strip away rhetorical tricks to ensure a paper never fakes a citation. We have systems like Mechanus that can dive into a matrix of billions of</v>

206
00:22:25.797 --> 00:22:32.821
<v Maya>numbers, locate the 0.05% of parameters where an AI forms a belief, and rewrite its cognitive behavior on the fly.</v>

207
00:22:32.841 --> 00:22:36.023
<v Daniel>The mechanical execution of the scientific method is being completely commodified.</v>

208
00:22:36.308 --> 00:22:48.594
<v Maya>But on the other hand, when you give these same systems six days and $3,000 to do open-ended research, they fail completely to grasp what actually makes a scientific contribution valuable.</v>

209
00:22:48.814 --> 00:22:49.695
<v Daniel>They don't know what matters.</v>

210
00:22:49.955 --> 00:23:04.722
<v Maya>And this brings us to a philosophical question that is currently raging in the news pages of Nature this very month. If an AI can perfectly format the document, perfectly retrieve the literature, and perfectly execute the statistical test, But it cannot</v>

211
00:23:04.782 --> 00:23:10.446
<v Maya>tell whether the question was worth answering in the first place. What exactly is left that we call research?</v>

212
00:23:10.866 --> 00:23:14.168
<v Daniel>It forces us to redefine what a scientist actually is.</v>

213
00:23:14.608 --> 00:23:29.514
<v Maya>We started by asking if human scientists are obsolete. But maybe the truth is that the true essence of a scientist is no longer the ability to find the answers. Think about what this means for you the next time you use an AI tool to solve a problem. The machine can build the x-ray machine</v>

214
00:23:29.554 --> 00:23:36.696
<v Maya>and print the film, but you still need the human to look at the murky, complicated picture and decide what it actually means for the patient.</v>

215
00:23:36.856 --> 00:23:39.257
<v Daniel>A perfect artifact still requires a worthy spark.</v>

216
00:23:39.637 --> 00:23:45.359
<v Maya>And right now, that spark is still unequivocally human. Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.</v>
